<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<quiz>

<!-- ================================================================
     REFERENCE PROTOTYPE: ANTIDIFFERENTIATION (indefinite integral)
     Core 2, Chapter 9 (Differentiation and Integration).

     This is the CANONICAL pattern to copy for EVERY Core 2 indefinite
     integration question. It is NOT live student content.

     WHY THIS DESIGN (read before copying):

     Correctness is established by the *antiderivative relationship*,
     not by string-matching one particular form. The STACK built-in
     "Int" answer test (ATInt) does the work in a single node:

       - It DIFFERENTIATES the student's answer and checks the result
         equals the integrand, so any algebraically equivalent
         antiderivative is accepted (this is the verification node, in
         the spirit of Core 1 Ch10 A2's manual AlgEquiv(diff(ans1,x),
         integrand) trick, but more robust).
       - By default it is fussy about the constant of integration
         (constint:true), so it REQUIRES "+ c" and accepts any constant
         name (c, k, A, ...). Omitting the constant fails the test.

     So node 0 (Int) does BOTH the verification and the constant-of-
     integration check, which the older Ch10 manual-diff trick could not.

     Diagnostics (the whole point of the prototype) hang off the false
     path of node 0, using AlgEquiv to name the specific error:
       node 1 - correct antiderivative but no "+ c"  -> "add + c";
       node 2 - differentiated instead of integrated -> shows the rule;
       node 3 - raised each index but forgot to divide by the new index
                (named power-rule error);
       node 3 false - generic power-rule hint.

     INSTALL NOTES / QUIRKS (verified in our Maxima install):
       - The maxima functions live in stackmaxima.mac as ATInt,
         ATAntidiff, ATAddConst, ATDiff. The XML <answertest> names are
         Int, Antidiff, AddConst, Diff (no AT prefix).
       - Antidiff and AddConst set simp:false without making simp
         local, but a compiled PRT makes simp local and resets it after
         every answer test, so the leak stays inside the PRT (checked on
         STACK 4.13.1). Int is still the simpler choice here.
       - Int's options field = the variable of integration only (x here).
       - With quiet=1 the test's feedback is hidden, but its answer note
         is still recorded before the branch note; qtests compare the
         branch notes only.
     ================================================================ -->
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Integration with antidiff prototype - indefinite integral with diagnostics</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[
<p>Find the indefinite integral of \({@integrand@}\) with respect to \(x\). Remember the constant of integration.</p>
<p>Enter the constant of integration as <code>+ c</code>.</p>
<div>\(\displaystyle \int \left({@integrand@}\right)\,\mathrm{d}x =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</div>
    ]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[
<p>Integration is the reverse process of differentiation. To integrate a power of \(x\), increase the index by 1 and divide by the new index:</p>
<p>\[\int x^n \,\mathrm{d}x = \frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + c.\]</p>
<p>Applying this to each term:</p>
<p>\[\int \left({@integrand@}\right)\,\mathrm{d}x = \frac{{@a@}x^{3}}{3} + \frac{{@b@}x^{2}}{2} + c = {@F@} + c.\]</p>
<p>The constant of integration \(c\) is needed because differentiating any constant gives zero, so an indefinite integral is only determined up to an added constant.</p>
<p>Check: \(\dfrac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}\left({@F@} + c\right) = {@integrand@}.\) &#10003;</p>
    ]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026080600</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[
/* Design backwards: choose nice integer antiderivative coefficients,
   then build the integrand so every variant integrates cleanly.
   a is a multiple of 3 and b a multiple of 2 so a/3 and b/2 are integers.
   The canonical instance (3x^2 + 2x -> x^3 + x^2 + c) is the ca=cb=1 variant. */
ca: rand_with_step(1, 3, 1);
cb: rand_with_step(1, 3, 1);
a: 3*ca;
b: 2*cb;
integrand: a*x^2 + b*x;
F: ca*x^3 + cb*x^2;        /* model antiderivative, without the constant */
ta1: F + c;                /* full model answer (used for input display) */
ddx: diff(integrand, x);   /* = 2*a*x + b : the "differentiated instead" error */
power_err: a*x^3 + b*x^2;  /* raised each index but forgot to divide by the new index */
    ]]></text>
  </questionvariables>
  <specificfeedback format="html"><text>[[feedback:prt1]]</text></specificfeedback>
  <questionnote format="moodle_auto_format">
    <text>Integrand {@integrand@}; antiderivative {@F@} + c.</text>
  </questionnote>
  <questiondescription format="html"><text><![CDATA[This question is original to this kit and does not come from a source text. It is the reference example of an indefinite integral graded with the Int answer test, with diagnostics.]]></text></questiondescription>
  <questionsimplify>1</questionsimplify>
  <assumepositive>0</assumepositive>
  <assumereal>0</assumereal>
  <prtcorrect format="html"><text><![CDATA[[[commonstring key="symbolicprtcorrectfeedback"/]] [[commonstring key="defaultprtcorrectfeedback"/]]]]></text></prtcorrect>
  <prtpartiallycorrect format="html"><text><![CDATA[[[commonstring key="symbolicprtpartiallycorrectfeedback"/]] [[commonstring key="defaultprtpartiallycorrectfeedback"/]]]]></text></prtpartiallycorrect>
  <prtincorrect format="html"><text><![CDATA[[[commonstring key="symbolicprtincorrectfeedback"/]] [[commonstring key="defaultprtincorrectfeedback"/]]]]></text></prtincorrect>
  <decimals>.</decimals>
  <scientificnotation>*10</scientificnotation>
  <multiplicationsign>none</multiplicationsign>
  <sqrtsign>1</sqrtsign>
  <complexno>i</complexno>
  <inversetrig>cos-1</inversetrig>
  <logicsymbol>lang</logicsymbol>
  <matrixparens>[</matrixparens>
  <isbroken>0</isbroken>
  <variantsselectionseed></variantsselectionseed>

  <input><name>ans1</name><type>algebraic</type><tans>ta1</tans><boxsize>15</boxsize><strictsyntax>1</strictsyntax><insertstars>0</insertstars><syntaxhint></syntaxhint><syntaxattribute>0</syntaxattribute><forbidwords>int,integrate</forbidwords><allowwords></allowwords><forbidfloat>1</forbidfloat><requirelowestterms>0</requirelowestterms><checkanswertype>0</checkanswertype><mustverify>0</mustverify><showvalidation>3</showvalidation><options></options></input>

  <prt>
    <name>prt1</name>
    <value>1.0000000</value>
    <autosimplify>1</autosimplify>
    <feedbackstyle>1</feedbackstyle>
    <feedbackvariables><text></text></feedbackvariables>

    <!-- NODE 0: VERIFICATION + CONSTANT CHECK. Int differentiates the
         student's answer, checks it returns the integrand (any equivalent
         antiderivative is accepted), AND requires the constant of
         integration (constint:true by default). Any constant name works.
         Everything that fails routes to the diagnostics. -->
    <node>
      <name>0</name>
      <description>Int: antiderivative relationship and constant of integration</description>
      <answertest>Int</answertest>
      <sans>ans1</sans>
      <tans>F</tans>
      <testoptions>x</testoptions>
      <quiet>1</quiet>
      <truescoremode>=</truescoremode>
      <truescore>1.0000000</truescore>
      <truepenalty></truepenalty>
      <truenextnode>-1</truenextnode>
      <trueanswernote>prt1-1-T</trueanswernote>
      <truefeedback format="html"><text></text></truefeedback>
      <falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode>
      <falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore>
      <falsepenalty></falsepenalty>
      <falsenextnode>1</falsenextnode>
      <falseanswernote>prt1-1-F</falseanswernote>
      <falsefeedback format="html"><text></text></falsefeedback>
    </node>

    <!-- NODE 1: DIAGNOSTIC - correct antiderivative but constant omitted.
         Reached when Int failed; if the answer equals the antiderivative
         exactly then the only thing missing is the constant. -->
    <node>
      <name>1</name>
      <description>Diagnostic: antiderivative correct but no constant of integration</description>
      <answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest>
      <sans>ans1</sans>
      <tans>F</tans>
      <testoptions></testoptions>
      <quiet>1</quiet>
      <truescoremode>=</truescoremode>
      <truescore>0.0000000</truescore>
      <truepenalty></truepenalty>
      <truenextnode>-1</truenextnode>
      <trueanswernote>prt1-2-T</trueanswernote>
      <truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Your antiderivative is right, but you have omitted the constant of integration. Because differentiating a constant gives zero, the indefinite integral must include an arbitrary constant. Add \(+ c\).</p>]]></text></truefeedback>
      <falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode>
      <falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore>
      <falsepenalty></falsepenalty>
      <falsenextnode>2</falsenextnode>
      <falseanswernote>prt1-2-F</falseanswernote>
      <falsefeedback format="html"><text></text></falsefeedback>
    </node>

    <!-- NODE 2: DIAGNOSTIC - differentiated instead of integrated. -->
    <node>
      <name>2</name>
      <description>Diagnostic: differentiated the integrand instead of integrating</description>
      <answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest>
      <sans>ans1</sans>
      <tans>ddx</tans>
      <testoptions></testoptions>
      <quiet>1</quiet>
      <truescoremode>=</truescoremode>
      <truescore>0.0000000</truescore>
      <truepenalty></truepenalty>
      <truenextnode>-1</truenextnode>
      <trueanswernote>prt1-3-T</trueanswernote>
      <truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>It looks like you differentiated rather than integrated. To integrate a power, increase the index by 1 and divide by the new index: \(\displaystyle\int x^n\,\mathrm{d}x = \frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + c\).</p>]]></text></truefeedback>
      <falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode>
      <falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore>
      <falsepenalty></falsepenalty>
      <falsenextnode>3</falsenextnode>
      <falseanswernote>prt1-3-F</falseanswernote>
      <falsefeedback format="html"><text></text></falsefeedback>
    </node>

    <!-- NODE 3: DIAGNOSTIC - raised the index but forgot to divide by it. -->
    <node>
      <name>3</name>
      <description>Diagnostic: raised each index but did not divide by the new index</description>
      <answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest>
      <sans>ans1</sans>
      <tans>power_err</tans>
      <testoptions></testoptions>
      <quiet>1</quiet>
      <truescoremode>=</truescoremode>
      <truescore>0.0000000</truescore>
      <truepenalty></truepenalty>
      <truenextnode>-1</truenextnode>
      <trueanswernote>prt1-4-T</trueanswernote>
      <truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>You increased each index by 1, but you must also divide by the new index. For example, \(\displaystyle\int {@a@}x^{2}\,\mathrm{d}x = \frac{{@a@}x^{3}}{3} = {@ca@}x^{3}\), not \({@a@}x^{3}\). Do the same for every term, and add \(+ c\).</p>]]></text></truefeedback>
      <falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode>
      <falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore>
      <falsepenalty></falsepenalty>
      <falsenextnode>-1</falsenextnode>
      <falseanswernote>prt1-4-F</falseanswernote>
      <falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>To integrate each power of \(x\), increase the index by 1, divide by the new index, then add the constant of integration: \(\displaystyle\int x^n\,\mathrm{d}x = \frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + c\). Check each term and try again.</p>]]></text></falsefeedback>
    </node>
  </prt>

  <qtest>
    <testcase>1</testcase>
    <description>Correct: antiderivative with constant c -> full marks</description>
    <testinput><name>ans1</name><value>F + c</value></testinput>
    <expected><name>prt1</name><expectedscore>1.0000000</expectedscore><expectedpenalty>0.0000000</expectedpenalty><expectedanswernote>prt1-1-T</expectedanswernote></expected>
  </qtest>
  <qtest>
    <testcase>2</testcase>
    <description>Correct with a different constant name k -> full marks (any constant accepted)</description>
    <testinput><name>ans1</name><value>F + k</value></testinput>
    <expected><name>prt1</name><expectedscore>1.0000000</expectedscore><expectedpenalty>0.0000000</expectedpenalty><expectedanswernote>prt1-1-T</expectedanswernote></expected>
  </qtest>
  <qtest>
    <testcase>3</testcase>
    <description>Forgot the constant of integration -> 0, hits +c diagnostic</description>
    <testinput><name>ans1</name><value>F</value></testinput>
    <expected><name>prt1</name><expectedscore>0.0000000</expectedscore><expectedpenalty>0.1000000</expectedpenalty><expectedanswernote>prt1-1-F | prt1-2-T</expectedanswernote></expected>
  </qtest>
  <qtest>
    <testcase>4</testcase>
    <description>Differentiated instead of integrated -> 0, hits differentiated diagnostic</description>
    <testinput><name>ans1</name><value>ddx</value></testinput>
    <expected><name>prt1</name><expectedscore>0.0000000</expectedscore><expectedpenalty>0.1000000</expectedpenalty><expectedanswernote>prt1-1-F | prt1-2-F | prt1-3-T</expectedanswernote></expected>
  </qtest>
  <qtest>
    <testcase>5</testcase>
    <description>Raised index but did not divide by new index -> 0, hits power-rule diagnostic</description>
    <testinput><name>ans1</name><value>power_err</value></testinput>
    <expected><name>prt1</name><expectedscore>0.0000000</expectedscore><expectedpenalty>0.1000000</expectedpenalty><expectedanswernote>prt1-1-F | prt1-2-F | prt1-3-F | prt1-4-T</expectedanswernote></expected>
  </qtest>
  <qtest>
    <testcase>6</testcase>
    <description>Completely wrong (not an antiderivative) -> 0, generic hint</description>
    <testinput><name>ans1</name><value>x^99</value></testinput>
    <expected><name>prt1</name><expectedscore>0.0000000</expectedscore><expectedpenalty>0.1000000</expectedpenalty><expectedanswernote>prt1-1-F | prt1-2-F | prt1-3-F | prt1-4-F</expectedanswernote></expected>
  </qtest>
</question>

</quiz>
